   text head: 'The sky appears blue because sunlight is made of all colors, and as it passes through the atmosphere, air molecules scatter shorter wavelengths (blue and violet) more strongly than longer ones. This effect, called Rayleigh scattering, is stronger for blue because its wavelength is closer to the size of the molecules doing the scattering. Our eyes are also more sensitive to blue than violet, and some violet is absorbed higher up, so the sky looks blue rather than purple.<|user|>'
